::ENG2_U04_L07_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Source finding: A district pilot report found fewer unwanted trays after staff prepared meals from student pre-order selections. Which sentence is most publication-ready?{
=A. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays after staff prepared meals from student pre-order selections.#Correct. This sentence is accurate, specific, and source-based.
~B. The lunch thing helped stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~C. Forms fixed every waste problem forever.#Incorrect. This overstates the evidence.
~D. Students should just do the form.#Incorrect. This gives an action but does not explain the evidence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L07_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which visual best supports the source-based message?{
~A. A random cafeteria photo with no label.#Incorrect. A visual must support the evidence.
=B. A labeled before-and-after chart showing unwanted trays before and after the pilot.#Correct. This visual directly helps the audience understand the source finding.
~C. A decorative border around the title.#Incorrect. Decoration does not explain evidence.
~D. A school mascot image with no connection to lunch forms.#Incorrect. This visual is unrelated to the message.
}

::ENG2_U04_L07_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best explains why the evidence matters?{
~A. Evidence is always important.#Incorrect. This does not explain this specific evidence.
~B. The report exists.#Incorrect. This only mentions the source.
=C. This finding matters because student selections helped staff prepare meals students were more likely to choose.#Correct. This explains how the evidence connects to the purpose.
~D. Lunch is a school subject.#Incorrect. This is unrelated to the evidence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L07_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is complete and clear?{
~A. Because the publication needs a source.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~B. The chart, the report, students.#Incorrect. This is not a complete sentence.
~C. When students forms.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
=D. The publication should include accurate evidence, clear explanation, and a visual that supports the message.#Correct. This is complete and clear.
}

::ENG2_U04_L07_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which final checklist best fits publication-ready communication?{
~A. Add a picture, make it colorful, and submit quickly.#Incorrect. This does not check accuracy or audience needs.
=B. Check source accuracy, explanation, audience wording, visual support, and conventions.#Correct. These checks match the lesson goal.
~C. Use only opinions and no evidence.#Incorrect. The communication must be source-based.
~D. Copy the source without explaining it.#Incorrect. Publication-ready work explains evidence for the audience.
}
